ERROR REPORTS

Please send errors and suggestions to Frans Kaashoek and Robert Morris
(kaashoek,rtm@mit.edu).  The main purpose of xv6 is as a teaching
operating system for MIT's 6.1810, so we are more interested in
simplifications and clarifications than new features.

BUILDING AND RUNNING XV6

You will need a RISC-V "newlib" tool chain from
https://github.com/riscv/riscv-gnu-toolchain, and qemu compiled for
riscv64-softmmu.  Once they are installed, and in your shell
search path, you can run "make qemu".


WRITE UP
Replaced the round robin with the requested stride scheduler

runtime - how many times a process was scheduled
stride - the priority of the process
pass - decide which process goes next
runtime, stride, pass were added to proc.h
the scheduler was alowayds select hte process with lowest pass value

method implemented were getpinfo and setstride 

Files that were modiefied

kernel/proc.h --> runtime, pass, stride were added
kernel/proc.c
-initialize runtime, pass, stride 
-updated the scheduler() method to pick the proccesss that has the lowest pass value
-Global variables MaxPassTotal so that when the passTottal is larger than MaxPassTotal then the pass value will reset
kernel/pstat.h
Added
  int runtime[NPROC];   // the number of ticks each process has accumulated 
  int pass[NPROC];
  int passTotal;
setStride was added 
kernel/sysproc.c
-added sysstride 
kernal/syscall.h
-added a syscall number for sys_setStride
kernel/syscall.c
-added the entry for sys_setStride
user/getpinfo.c
-allows us to know what the scheduler is doing like which process is running
user/usys.pl
-added entry("setStride");
user/hardwork.c
-takes in a value stride value and the way we test the scheduler



